Common rather than popular.  Given the word pair famous
and infamous there should be a word pair like popular
inpopular.  Using regular English words maybe "infamously
popular".

The majority of monster types are at-depth for their
numerical difficulty so as a player progresses the game
attempts to match the hit point appropriate to your XP
level or dungeon level.  Adding AC biases this to make
the monsters easier as you progress deeper*.  Some
monsters are powerfull out of depth for various reasons.
Spell casters because they can delete your armor items
or curse your items so you can't switch weapons or
summon swarms of nasty monsters.  Trappers and worms
that can instakill non-player monsters, mind flayers
and so on.

The early out of depth monsters are the types that are
created in swarms.  Any one orc is trivial in the first
few levels, but they are created in small swarms.  Any
one soldier ant should be a reasonable match when they
first appear, but they come in swarms not individuals
and they are fast at a time when most characters aren't.
With orcs they are slow enough that a player usually
has time to retreat to a corridor.  With soldier ants
they are fast enough that a player can easily be caught
in a large room without enough time to reach a corridor.

To see how easy individual solder ants are to handle,
run a wizard who starts with "create monster" spell**
and do a sacfest until you've gotten a bunch of solider
ants some time.  One at a time they aren't a problem.

* An idea that has been mentioned before - Those
monsters that are created with armor, enchant that armor
to a level appropriate to the current difficulty.  This
would make certain monster types *far* more dangerous,
especially the soldiers in the two castles.

** Make the create monster spell creates swarms of
swarm-default monsters.



